    {
      "id": "d0dJnv4pFnQgKZFi936NW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 414.4250681601383,
      "y": 1312.1743589296507,
      "width": 527.6033945979732,
      "height": 2725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aY",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1989488607,
      "version": 2677,
      "versionNonce": 1007247263,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793376921284,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Adjacent NODES\n---------------------------\n\nIf a vertex is reachable directly from another vertex\nthen it is said to be adjacent to that vertex .\n\nex - V1 is adjacent to V2 but V2 is not adjacent to\nV1 .\n\n\n2. Directed/Undirected Edge\n--------------------------------------\n\nIf an edge as direction we say it is directed\notherwise we say it is undirected .\n\n \n3. Directed/ Undirected Graph\n-----------------------------------------\n\nIf in a Graph all the edges has a direction then we\nsay it is a directed graph .\n\nIf no edges in graph has direction then we say it is\nundirected graph .\n\n\n4. Mixed Graph\n--------------------\n\nIf in a graph some edges are directed and some are\nnot directed then we say graph is mixed graph .\n\n\n5. Initial & Terminal Vertices\n---------------------------------------\n\nThe vertex from which edge originates is called as\ninitial vertex and vertex where edge terminates is\ncalled as Terminal vertex . For example : for e1 in our\ngraph the vertex v2 is initial vertex and v1 is\nterminal vertex .\n\n\n6.SLING\n-----------\n\nIf in a graph we have same initial and same terminal\nvertex then edge is said to be SLING (self loop) in\nour graph e5 is sling edge .\n\n\n7. Parallel edges\n----------------------\n\nIf we have more than one egde connecting 2 vertices\nthen we say that these are parallel edges . Then\ngraph is also called as MULTI-GRAPH .\n\n\n8. Weighted Graph\n-------------------------\n\nIf every edge in a graph has positive number\nassigned to it then we call it is a weighted graph .\n\n\n9. Tree Graph\n--------------------\n\nIf in a Graph we do not have any closed path then\nwe say that it is a tree graph .\n\n\n10. INDEGREE\n---------------------\n\nNumber of edges terminating at a vertex is called as\nits indegree .\n\nex - indegree of v1 is 3 and of v2 is 0 .\n\n\n11. OUTDEGREE\n-----------------------\n\nNumber of edges originating from a vertex is called\nits outdegree .\n\nex - outdegree of v1 is 1 . outdegree of v2 is 2 .\n\n\n12. SOURCE\n-----------------\n\nIf in a graph we have a vertex with 0 in degree but\npositive outdegree then it said to be source and\ntraversal must start with soucre , otherwise we\nwon't be able to access that vertex . In our graph\nv2 is source vertex .\n\n\n13. SINK\n------------\n\nIn a graph we have a vertex with +ve INDEGREE\nbut 0 outdegree then we say it is a SINK vertex .\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Adjacent NODES\n---------------------------\n\nIf a vertex is reachable directly from another vertex then it is said to be adjacent to that vertex .\n\nex - V1 is adjacent to V2 but V2 is not adjacent to V1 .\n\n\n2. Directed/Undirected Edge\n--------------------------------------\n\nIf an edge as direction we say it is directed otherwise we say it is undirected .\n\n \n3. Directed/ Undirected Graph\n-----------------------------------------\n\nIf in a Graph all the edges has a direction then we say it is a directed graph .\n\nIf no edges in graph has direction then we say it is undirected graph .\n\n\n4. Mixed Graph\n--------------------\n\nIf in a graph some edges are directed and some are not directed then we say graph is mixed graph .\n\n\n5. Initial & Terminal Vertices\n---------------------------------------\n\nThe vertex from which edge originates is called as initial vertex and vertex where edge terminates is called as Terminal vertex . For example : for e1 in our graph the vertex v2 is initial vertex and v1 is terminal vertex .\n\n\n6.SLING\n-----------\n\nIf in a graph we have same initial and same terminal vertex then edge is said to be SLING (self loop) in our graph e5 is sling edge .\n\n\n7. Parallel edges\n----------------------\n\nIf we have more than one egde connecting 2 vertices then we say that these are parallel edges . Then graph is also called as MULTI-GRAPH .\n\n\n8. Weighted Graph\n-------------------------\n\nIf every edge in a graph has positive number assigned to it then we call it is a weighted graph .\n\n\n9. Tree Graph\n--------------------\n\nIf in a Graph we do not have any closed path then we say that it is a tree graph .\n\n\n10. INDEGREE\n---------------------\n\nNumber of edges terminating at a vertex is called as its indegree .\n\nex - indegree of v1 is 3 and of v2 is 0 .\n\n\n11. OUTDEGREE\n-----------------------\n\nNumber of edges originating from a vertex is called its outdegree .\n\nex - outdegree of v1 is 1 . outdegree of v2 is 2 .\n\n\n12. SOURCE\n-----------------\n\nIf in a graph we have a vertex with 0 in degree but positive outdegree then it said to be source and traversal must start with soucre , otherwise we won't be able to access that vertex . In our graph v2 is source vertex .\n\n\n13. SINK\n------------\n\nIn a graph we have a vertex with +ve INDEGREE but 0 outdegree then we say it is a SINK vertex .\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
